artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a bounded convex domain with smooth boundary $\partial\Omega$, and let $H \in \mathbb{R}$ be a constant. Consider the constant-mean-curvature Dirichlet problem
$$\operatorname{div}\!\left(\frac{Du}{\sqrt{1+|Du|^2}}\right) = 2H \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega,$$
whose solutions are graphs $z = u(x,y)$ of constant mean curvature $H$ spanning $\partial\Omega$. Denote by $|\Omega|$ the area of $\Omega$, by $L$ the length of $\partial\Omega$, and by $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega)$ its Euclidean diameter. Determine whether the Dirichlet problem above admits a solution under each one of the following hypotheses separately:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $|\Omega|\,H^2 < \pi$;
\item $L\,H^2 < 2\pi$;
\item $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega) < 2/|H|$.
\end{enumerate}
That is, decide, in each of these three regimes, whether solvability of the Dirichlet problem can be guaranteed for every such bounded convex domain $\Omega$.

